@page

<p>
    HTML is what is on the page. <strong>CSS</strong> (Cascading Style Sheets) is how it looks:
    colors, fonts, sizes, and spacing, etc.
</p>

<h2>Parts of CSS</h2>
<pre>
h1 {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 40px;
}
</pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>h1</code> is the <strong>selector</strong>. It selects which of the tags to edit.</li>
    <li><code>color</code> and <code>font-size</code> are <strong>properties which change how the tags look</strong>.</li>
    <li><code>blue</code> and <code>40px</code> are <strong>values which determine how much it should change the tags</strong>.</li>
    <li>And every line ends with a semicolon like in C#.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Selectors</h2>
<pre>
/* Every paragraph */
p {
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* Only tags with class="note" */
.note {
  background-color: lightyellow;
}

/* Links when the mouse is over them */
a:hover {
  color: red;
}
</pre>
<p>
    A selector with a dot in front of it like <code>.note</code>, is a <strong>class selector</strong>. You add
    <code>class="note"</code> to an HTML tag and edit how it looks with CSS. For example, the yellow boxes in
    this website use the <code>note</code> class.
</p>

<h2>Adding Colors</h2>
<ul>
    <li>You can add color names, like <code>red</code> or <code>blue</code></li>
    <li>Also you can use hex codes, like <code>#2c5aa0</code>. which say how much red, green, and blue are inside the color you want.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A box model</h2>
<p>Your browser treats the site like a box with four layers:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Content</strong> - the things inside like the words or pictures</li>
    <li><strong>Padding</strong> - empty space inside of the border between the content and the border</li>
    <li><strong>Border</strong> - a line showing the edge of the page</li>
    <li><strong>Margin</strong> - space outside the border of the page</li>
</ol>

<h2>Where CSS is used in this website</h2>
<p>
    All the colors and shapes and fonts in this website use CSS. Like the yellow boxes surrouding some sentences or the headings.
</p>

